<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kostis Palamas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1859">1859</a>–<a href="/years/1943">1943</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kostis Palamas was a Greek poet, a leading figure of the New Athenian School and of the movement to use spoken demotic Greek in literature. His major works include The Twelve Words of the Gypsy and The King's Flute. He wrote the words of the Olympic Hymn for the 1896 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> Games, and his funeral in occupied Athens in 1943 became a patriotic demonstration.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kostis-palamas.jpg" alt="Kostis Palamas" width="320" height="119" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kostis Palamas (1859-1943) // Κωστής Παλαμάς (1859-1943) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kostis_Palamas_signature_1939.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
